WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.725
This final activity brings the module together into
one small PostgreSQL workflow

00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:10.212
Begin with the required path Write a SELECT
statement with an explicit

00:00:10.238 --> 00:00:14.762
column list Add one WHERE condition so the query
answers a specific

00:00:14.788 --> 00:00:20.425
question Sort the result so the output has a
meaningful order Next

00:00:20.575 --> 00:00:25.025
insert one test row and use RETURNING Then verify
that row with

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.125
a SELECT based on a stable key After that update the
row

00:00:29.138 --> 00:00:33.262
safely by targeting the same key and inspect the
result If you

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:37.275
are comfortable with the required steps try one or
more optional challenges

00:00:38.162 --> 00:00:42.200
Put a change inside a transaction and roll it back
Query a

00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:47.025
value from JSONB Create a small derived table with
INSERT INTO SELECT

00:00:47.912 --> 00:00:51.912
Or intentionally trigger a constraint error and
explain why the database rejected

00:00:51.938 --> 00:00:58.125
the operation The working method matters more than
completing everything quickly Start

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:03.950
from a known pattern Change one part Run it Read

00:01:03.975 --> 00:01:08.862
the output Only then add the next part This is a
strong

00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:14.312
general approach to data preparation Complex
workflows are easier to debug when

00:01:14.325 --> 00:01:18.812
each step is understandable and verifiable By the
end of this activity

00:01:19.062 --> 00:01:22.438
you should be able to move between reading data
filtering it changing

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.650
it carefully and checking that the database behaved
as intended
